-- title:
--   The recombining binomial tree, its survival probabilities $G_{i,j}$ and the distorted probability $\mathbb Q$
-- statement:
--   The discrete model of §3.2 of Ma, Wong and Zhang.
--
--   **The tree** (eq. (3.11)). Times $0=t_0<\dots<t_N$. At time $t_i$ the state $X_{t_i}$ takes values $x_{i,0}<\dots<x_{i,i}$, and
--   $$
--   \mathbb P\bigl(X_{t_{i+1}}=x_{i+1,j+1}\mid X_{t_i}=x_{i,j}\bigr)=p^+_{i,j},\qquad \mathbb P\bigl(X_{t_{i+1}}=x_{i+1,j}\mid X_{t_i}=x_{i,j}\bigr)=p^-_{i,j}:=1-p^+_{i,j},
--   $$
--   with $p^\pm_{i,j}>0$. The model is realized on the paths $\omega\in\{\text{up},\text{down}\}^N$: $X_{t_i}(\omega)=x_{i,\ell_i(\omega)}$ where $\ell_i(\omega)$ is the number of up-moves among the first $i$ moves, and a path has probability $\prod_m p^{\pm}_{m,\ell_m(\omega)}$. The filtration $\mathcal F_{t_i}=\sigma(X_{t_0},\dots,X_{t_i})$ is generated by the sets of paths that agree in their first $i$ moves.
--
--   **Survival probabilities** (p.12). $G_{i,j}:=\mathbb P(X_{t_i}\ge x_{i,j})$ for $0\le j\le i$, and $G_{i,i+1}:=0$.
--
--   **Initial distortions.** Given distortion functions $\varphi_{t_1},\dots,\varphi_{t_N}$, set $\varphi_{t_0}=\varphi_0(p):=p$.
--
--   **Distorted transition probabilities** (eq. (3.13)). For $0\le j\le i\le N-1$,
--   $$
--   q^+_{i,j}:=\frac{\varphi_{t_{i+1}}(G_{i+1,j+1})-\varphi_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1})}{\varphi_{t_i}(G_{i,j})-\varphi_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1})},\qquad q^-_{i,j}:=1-q^+_{i,j},
--   $$
--   and $\mathbb Q$ is the probability under which $X$ is Markov with these transition probabilities (eq. (3.15)).
--
--   **Condition (3.14).** $\varphi_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1})<\varphi_{t_{i+1}}(G_{i+1,j+1})<\varphi_{t_i}(G_{i,j})$ for $0\le j\le i\le N-1$.
--
--   **The recursion (3.16)** of Lemma 3.3. For $g\in\mathcal I$ and $0<n\le N$: $u_n(x):=g(x)$ and, for $i=n-1,\dots,0$, $u_i(x_{i,j}):=q^+_{i,j}u_{i+1}(x_{i+1,j+1})+q^-_{i,j}u_{i+1}(x_{i+1,j})$, $j=0,\dots,i$.
--
--   The module also provides probabilities, conditional probabilities $\mathbb P(A\mid B)=\mathbb P(A\cap B)/\mathbb P(B)$ and conditional expectations of functions of the path, under any family of up-probabilities ($p^+$ for $\mathbb P$, $q^+$ for $\mathbb Q$).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Paths are `Fin N → Bool`; times are indices; `x i j` and `p i j` are $x_{i,j}$ and $p^+_{i,j}$, with values outside $0\le j\le i$ irrelevant. Conditional probabilities and expectations divide by the probability of the conditioning event, which is positive for every event used here under $0<p^+_{i,j}<1$ (and under $\mathbb Q$ once $0<q^+_{i,j}<1$). $(3.13)$ is printed for $0\le j\le i\le N$ but uses $\varphi_{t_{i+1}}$, so only $i\le N-1$ is meaningful. `uLemma x q g n i j` is $u_i(x_{i,j})$.
-- source:
--   Ma–Wong–Zhang, Time-consistent conditional expectation under probability distortion, arXiv:1809.08262v5, https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.08262v5, §3.2, eqs. (3.11), (3.13), (3.14), p.12; eq. (3.15) and Lemma 3.3 (3.16), p.13

import Mathlib

open Set

namespace DistortedCondExp

/-- The path space of an `N`-period binomial tree (Ma–Wong–Zhang, *Time-consistent conditional
expectation under probability distortion*, arXiv:1809.08262v5, §3.2, p.12): a path records, for
each period `m = 0, …, N − 1`, whether the chain moves up (`true`) or down (`false`).

**Formalization Note** The paper works on an abstract filtered probability space carrying a
Markov process `X` with the transition law (3.11) and the filtration `F = F^X` it generates
(§2.2, p.7). Every quantity in the paper's §3.2 depends only on the law of `X` and on `F^X`, so
the canonical path space is used. The times `t_0 < ⋯ < t_N` enter only as labels and are
replaced by their indices `0, …, N`. -/
abbrev Path (N : ℕ) := Fin N → Bool

/-- The level of a path at time index `i`: the number of up-moves among the first `i` periods.
The chain sits at node `x_{i, lev i ω}` at time `t_i`. -/
def lev {N : ℕ} (i : ℕ) (ω : Path N) : ℕ :=
  (Finset.univ.filter fun m : Fin N => (m : ℕ) < i ∧ ω m = true).card

/-- The state `X_{t_i}(ω) = x_{i, lev i ω}` of the binomial tree (Ma–Wong–Zhang,
arXiv:1809.08262v5, §3.2, p.12): `x i j` is the node `x_{i,j}`, `0 ≤ j ≤ i`. -/
def X (x : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) {N : ℕ} (i : ℕ) (ω : Path N) : ℝ :=
  x i (lev i ω)

/-- The probability weight of a path when the chain moves from node `(m, j)` up with probability
`r m j` and down with probability `1 − r m j` (Ma–Wong–Zhang, arXiv:1809.08262v5, eq. (3.11),
p.12, with `r = p⁺`; and eq. (3.15), p.13, with `r = q⁺`). -/
def pathWeight (r : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) {N : ℕ} (ω : Path N) : ℝ :=
  ∏ m : Fin N, if ω m = true then r m (lev m ω) else 1 - r m (lev m ω)

open Classical in
/-- The probability of an event `A` of paths under the transition probabilities `r`:
`ℙ(A)` for `r = p⁺` ((3.11)), `ℚ(A)` for `r = q⁺` ((3.15)). -/
noncomputable def prob (r : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) {N : ℕ} (A : Set (Path N)) : ℝ :=
  ∑ ω ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun ω => ω ∈ A), pathWeight r ω

/-- The conditional probability `ℙ(A | B) = ℙ(A ∩ B) / ℙ(B)` of an event `A` given an event `B`
of positive probability, under the transition probabilities `r`.

**Formalization Note** Real division by zero is `0`; every conditioning event used in this
development (a node event `{X_{t_i} = x_{i,j}}` or an atom of `F_{t_i}`) has positive probability
under the standing hypothesis `0 < p⁺_{i,j} < 1`, and under `ℚ` once `0 < q⁺_{i,j} < 1`. -/
noncomputable def cprob (r : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) {N : ℕ} (A B : Set (Path N)) : ℝ :=
  prob r (A ∩ B) / prob r B

open Classical in
/-- The conditional expectation `E[f | B] = E[f 1_B] / ℙ(B)` of a function of the path given an
event `B` of positive probability, under the transition probabilities `r`. With `r = q⁺` this is
`𝔼^ℚ[f | B]`. -/
noncomputable def cexp (r : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) {N : ℕ} (f : Path N → ℝ) (B : Set (Path N)) : ℝ :=
  (∑ ω ∈ Finset.univ.filter (fun ω => ω ∈ B), pathWeight r ω * f ω) / prob r B

/-- The atom of `F_{t_s}` containing the path `ω`: the paths that agree with `ω` in the first `s`
periods.

**Formalization Note** `F_{t_s} = σ(X_{t_0}, …, X_{t_s})` (the filtration generated by `X`,
§2.2, p.7). Since `x_{i,0} < ⋯ < x_{i,i}` the levels `lev 0, …, lev s` are recovered from
`X_{t_0}, …, X_{t_s}`, and they determine the first `s` moves, so this `σ`-algebra is generated
by these atoms. Conditioning on `F_{t_s}` at `ω` is conditioning on `histAtom s ω`. -/
def histAtom {N : ℕ} (s : ℕ) (ω : Path N) : Set (Path N) :=
  {ω' | ∀ m : Fin N, (m : ℕ) < s → ω' m = ω m}

/-- The standing hypotheses on the recombining binomial tree (Ma–Wong–Zhang,
arXiv:1809.08262v5, §3.2, eq. (3.11), p.12): "for each `i = 0, …, N`, `X_{t_i}` takes values
`x_{i,0} < ⋯ < x_{i,i}`", and the up-probabilities satisfy `p^±_{i,j} > 0`, i.e.
`0 < p⁺_{i,j} < 1`, for `0 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ N − 1`.

**Formalization Note** `x i j` is the node `x_{i,j}` (any real number) and `p i j` is `p⁺_{i,j}`;
values of `x` and `p` outside the index ranges are irrelevant. -/
def IsBinomialTree (N : ℕ) (x p : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ i, i ≤ N → StrictMonoOn (x i) (Iic i)) ∧
  ∀ i j, j ≤ i → i < N → 0 < p i j ∧ p i j < 1

open Classical in
/-- The survival probabilities `G_{i,j} := ℙ(X_{t_i} ≥ x_{i,j})` of the binomial tree, with
`G_{i,i+1} := 0` (Ma–Wong–Zhang, arXiv:1809.08262v5, after eq. (3.13), p.12).

**Formalization Note** For `j ≤ i` the value is `ℙ{X_{t_i} ≥ x_{i,j}}` under the tree's law; for
`j > i` it is `0` (the paper sets only `G_{i,i+1} := 0`, and no other index is used). -/
noncomputable def G (N : ℕ) (x p : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (i j : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  if j ≤ i then prob p {ω : Path N | x i j ≤ X x i ω} else 0

/-- The family of initial distortion functions with `φ_{t_0} = φ_0 := id` (Ma–Wong–Zhang,
arXiv:1809.08262v5, p.12: "We assume further that … `ϕ_0(p) := p`").

**Formalization Note** The given family is `φ : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ`, `φ n = φ_{t_n}` for `1 ≤ n ≤ N`; its
value at `0` is ignored and replaced by the identity here. -/
def phiExt (φ : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ) (i : ℕ) : ℝ → ℝ :=
  if i = 0 then id else φ i

/-- The distorted up-probabilities (Ma–Wong–Zhang, arXiv:1809.08262v5, eq. (3.13), p.12):
`q⁺_{i,j} := (φ_{t_{i+1}}(G_{i+1,j+1}) − φ_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1})) / (φ_{t_i}(G_{i,j}) − φ_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1}))`,
`q⁻_{i,j} := 1 − q⁺_{i,j}`, with `φ_{t_0} = id` and `G_{i,i+1} = 0`. The probability `ℚ` of (3.15)
is `prob (qPlus N x p φ)`: the path law under which `X` is Markov with up-probabilities `q⁺`.

**Formalization Note** The paper prints the range `0 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ N`, but the formula needs
`φ_{t_{i+1}}`, so only `0 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ N − 1` is meaningful; other indices are never used. The
denominator is positive under (3.14) (`Cond314`). -/
noncomputable def qPlus (N : ℕ) (x p : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (φ : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ) (i j : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  (phiExt φ (i + 1) (G N x p (i + 1) (j + 1)) - phiExt φ i (G N x p i (j + 1))) /
    (phiExt φ i (G N x p i j) - phiExt φ i (G N x p i (j + 1)))

/-- Condition (3.14) (Ma–Wong–Zhang, arXiv:1809.08262v5, p.12):
`φ_{t_i}(G_{i,j+1}) < φ_{t_{i+1}}(G_{i+1,j+1}) < φ_{t_i}(G_{i,j})` for all `(i, j)`, i.e. for
`0 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ N − 1`, with `φ_{t_0} = id` and `G_{i,i+1} = 0`. -/
def Cond314 (N : ℕ) (x p : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (φ : ℕ → ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ i j, j ≤ i → i < N →
    phiExt φ i (G N x p i (j + 1)) < phiExt φ (i + 1) (G N x p (i + 1) (j + 1)) ∧
      phiExt φ (i + 1) (G N x p (i + 1) (j + 1)) < phiExt φ i (G N x p i j)

/-- Auxiliary for `uLemma`: `uAux x q g n d j` is `u_{n−d}(x_{n−d,j})`, computed by `d` steps of
the backward recursion (3.16) from `u_n = g`. -/
noncomputable def uAux (x q : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (g : ℝ → ℝ) (n : ℕ) : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ
  | 0, j => g (x n j)
  | d + 1, j =>
      q (n - (d + 1)) j * uAux x q g n d (j + 1) + (1 - q (n - (d + 1)) j) * uAux x q g n d j

/-- The backward recursion (3.16) of Lemma 3.3 (Ma–Wong–Zhang, arXiv:1809.08262v5, p.13):
`u_n(x) := g(x)` and, for `i = n − 1, …, 0`,
`u_i(x_{i,j}) := q⁺_{i,j} u_{i+1}(x_{i+1,j+1}) + q⁻_{i,j} u_{i+1}(x_{i+1,j})`, `j = 0, …, i`.

**Formalization Note** `uLemma x q g n i j` is the value `u_i(x_{i,j})` at the node `(i, j)`, for
`i ≤ n`; `q` is the family of up-probabilities (`q⁺` in the paper) and `q⁻ = 1 − q⁺`. Values for
`i > n` are not meaningful. -/
noncomputable def uLemma (x q : ℕ → ℕ → ℝ) (g : ℝ → ℝ) (n i j : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  uAux x q g n (n - i) j

end DistortedCondExp


